{#
  Rendered by the vmagent role — do not edit on the box. Edit the Monitoring Agent (or Grove
  Settings) and hit Install.

  Two ways to learn what to scrape, chosen by the agent's Push Targets Manually box:

  http_sd (default) — both jobs ask Grove directly and re-ask on the refresh interval, so a
  deploy or a new box lands here with nothing to re-run.

  file_sd — Grove writes the lists to this box instead, for a Grove the box cannot reach (a
  developer's laptop). Same entries, same labels; the difference is that they are a snapshot
  and go stale until Push Targets is pressed again.

  Either way the labels (machine, region, server, and `engine` verbatim) arrive with each
  target, so nothing on this box has to be kept in step with the fleet by hand.

  The SD token rides in the query string, not an Authorization header: Frappe's validate_auth
  rejects any header it cannot resolve to a user before the endpoint runs, so a bare shared
  secret cannot be sent that way. That puts a live credential in this file — hence its 0600 —
  and in Grove's own access log on every fetch.

  How often the lists are re-fetched is NOT here: vmagent rejects Prometheus's per-config
  `refresh_interval` on an http_sd_config and takes -promscrape.httpSDCheckInterval instead,
  set in the unit. Both jobs therefore share one interval.

  Every target is https now, on each box's TLS front, and every certificate there is self-signed
  — so both jobs skip verification. That encrypts the scrape; it does not authenticate the box.

  Only the host job authenticates: /metrics/node and /metrics/gpu sit behind basic auth, because
  an exporter answers anyone who reaches it. An engine's /metrics is inside its own
  /e/<slug>/ location and is guarded by nothing, exactly as it was when the engine port itself
  was open — vLLM's auth middleware covers /v1 and not /metrics.
#}
global:
  scrape_interval: {{ monitoring_scrape_interval }}

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: grove-host
    tls_config:
      insecure_skip_verify: true
    basic_auth:
      username: {{ monitoring_scrape_username }}
      # Not inline: /proc/<pid>/cmdline is world-readable and Ansible records what it runs on the
      # Ansible Task doc. The file is 0600.
      password_file: {{ vmagent_config_dir }}/scrape.password
{% if monitoring_static_targets %}
    file_sd_configs:
      - files: ["{{ vmagent_config_dir }}/targets_host.json"]
{% else %}
    http_sd_configs:
      - url: "{{ monitoring_sd_url }}?agent={{ monitoring_agent | urlencode }}&kind=host&token={{ monitoring_sd_token | urlencode }}"
{% endif %}

  - job_name: grove-engine
    scrape_interval: {{ monitoring_engine_scrape_interval }}
    tls_config:
      insecure_skip_verify: true
{% if monitoring_static_targets %}
    file_sd_configs:
      - files: ["{{ vmagent_config_dir }}/targets_engine.json"]
{% else %}
    http_sd_configs:
      - url: "{{ monitoring_sd_url }}?agent={{ monitoring_agent | urlencode }}&kind=engine&token={{ monitoring_sd_token | urlencode }}"
{% endif %}
